    stem: 'A 22-year-old woman presents to the student health clinic for evaluation of episodic mild jaundice that occurs during viral illnesses and periods of examination-related sleep deprivation. She is asymptomatic and denies pruritus, dark urine, pale stools, or abdominal pain. Physical examination reveals mild scleral icterus without hepatosplenomegaly or stigmata of chronic liver disease. Laboratory testing reveals: total bilirubin 4.2 mg/dL, direct (conjugated) bilirubin 2.6 mg/dL (predominantly conjugated hyperbilirubinemia; normal direct: < 0.3 mg/dL), AST 24 U/L, ALT 22 U/L, alkaline phosphatase 68 U/L, and normal serum albumin and INR. Urinary bilirubin is positive. A percutaneous liver biopsy is performed during an unrelated laparoscopic cholecystectomy for asymptomatic gallstones, revealing normal lobular architecture but striking, dark-brown granular pigment within centrilobular hepatocytes that stains positive on Fontana-Masson and is confined within lysosomes. Which of the following cellular transport mechanisms is primarily defective in this patient?',
    choices: [
      ['ATP-dependent canalicular export of conjugated bilirubin glucuronides via MRP2', 'Dubin-Johnson syndrome is an autosomal recessive benign disorder caused by mutations in the ABCC2 gene, which encodes the canalicular multidrug resistance-associated protein 2 (MRP2). MRP2 is an ATP-binding cassette transporter responsible for pumping conjugated bilirubin and other organic anions across the hepatocyte canalicular membrane into bile. Defective MRP2 leads to reflux of conjugated bilirubin into sinusoidal blood, causing direct hyperbilirubinemia and bilirubinuria. Impaired canalicular excretion of epinephrine metabolites leads to dense, dark-brown pigment accumulation in lysosomes, giving the liver a characteristic gross black appearance. Normal liver enzymes and histology distinguish it from hepatobiliary disease.'],
      ['UDP-glucuronosyltransferase-mediated conjugation of bilirubin with glucuronic acid in the ER', 'Gilbert syndrome and Crigler-Najjar syndrome are caused by decreased activity of UDP-glucuronosyltransferase 1A1 (UGT1A1). They produce UNCONJUGATED (indirect) hyperbilirubinemia without bilirubinuria or dark hepatic lysosomal pigment.'],
      ['Organic anion-transporting polypeptide (OATP1B1/1B3) sinusoidal uptake of bilirubin', 'Rotor syndrome is caused by deficiency of the sinusoidal uptake transporters OATP1B1 and OATP1B3 (SLCO1B1 and SLCO1B3). While it also causes conjugated hyperbilirubinemia, the liver in Rotor syndrome has completely NORMAL pigmentation without dark lysosomal granules.'],
      ['Bile salt export pump (BSEP/ABCB11) transport of bile acids across canalicular membranes', 'Progressive familial intrahepatic cholestasis type 2 (PFIC2) involves BSEP/ABCB11 mutations, causing severe progressive cholestasis, intractable pruritus, elevated bile acids, and cirrhosis in childhood.'],
      ['Copper-transporting P-type ATPase (ATP7B) translocation of copper into trans-Golgi vesicles', 'Wilson disease involves ATP7B mutations, causing hepatic copper accumulation, basal ganglia degeneration, Kayser-Fleischer rings, and low ceruloplasmin, not isolated conjugated hyperbilirubinemia with black liver.'],
    ],
    explanation: 'Dubin-Johnson syndrome is an autosomal recessive conjugated hyperbilirubinemia caused by mutations in the ABCC2 gene encoding MRP2 (multidrug resistance-associated protein 2), the canalicular transporter for conjugated bilirubin. Lysosomal accumulation of epinephrine metabolites produces the pathognomonic grossly black liver and microscopic dark granular pigment. In contrast, Rotor syndrome causes conjugated hyperbilirubinemia with normal liver histology/color, and Gilbert/Crigler-Najjar cause unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia.',
    objective: 'Identify the MRP2 canalicular transporter defect (ABCC2) and lysosomal dark pigment as characteristic of Dubin-Johnson syndrome.',
    takeaways: [
      'Dubin-Johnson: defective MRP2 canalicular transporter -> direct hyperbilirubinemia with black liver / lysosomal pigment.',
      'Rotor syndrome: defective OATP1B1/OATP1B3 sinusoidal uptake -> direct hyperbilirubinemia with normal histology and NO pigment.',
    ],
    references: [
      { label: 'Hepatology Communications 2021 Inherited Disorders of Bilirubin Clearance and Transport', url: 'https://aasldpubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/journal/2471254x' },
    ],
    shortestValidSolution: 'Conjugated hyperbilirubinemia + dark granular lysosomal liver pigment = Dubin-Johnson syndrome -> defective canalicular MRP2 transporter.',
    necessaryInference: 'Use the presence of dark lysosomal liver pigment to differentiate Dubin-Johnson (MRP2 defect) from Rotor syndrome (OATP defect, no pigment).',
    strongestDistractor: 'Rotor syndrome (OATP1B1/OATP1B3 defect), which also causes benign conjugated hyperbilirubinemia.',
