    difficulty: 4,
    stem: 'A 64-year-old man hospitalized in the intensive care unit for methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA) bacteremia secondary to a central venous catheter infection is treated with an intravenous lipopeptide antimicrobial agent. Three days into therapy, his blood cultures clear, but routine laboratory monitoring reveals a serum creatine kinase (CK) elevation to 1,840 U/L (normal: < 200 U/L) accompanied by bilateral proximal lower extremity myalgias. Serum creatinine is stable at 1.1 mg/dL. Which of the following molecular mechanisms describes the antimicrobial action of the therapeutic agent responsible for this adverse effect?',
    choices: [
      ['Calcium-dependent insertion of a lipophilic tail into the bacterial cytoplasmic membrane forming transmembrane channels and causing rapid potassium efflux', 'Daptomycin is a cyclic lipopeptide active exclusively against Gram-positive organisms, including MRSA and vancomycin-resistant enterococci (VRE). Its mechanism requires calcium ions, which allow its lipophilic hydrocarbon tail to insert into the bacterial cytoplasmic membrane. Oligomerization creates transmembrane pores that trigger rapid potassium efflux, causing rapid membrane depolarization, arrest of DNA/RNA/protein synthesis, and bacterial cell death without cell lysis. Its major dose-dependent toxicity is skeletal muscle toxicity (myalgia and rhabdomyolysis), mandating weekly monitoring of creatine kinase (CK). Furthermore, daptomycin is inactivated by pulmonary surfactant and cannot be used for pneumonia.'],
      ['Binding to the lipid A component of lipopolysaccharide in outer membranes acting as a cationic detergent', 'Polymyxins (colistin and polymyxin B) bind to lipid A on the outer membrane of Gram-NEGATIVE bacteria, disrupting membrane integrity. They cause nephrotoxicity and neurotoxicity, not isolated Gram-positive myopathy.'],
      ['Binding to the D-Ala-D-Ala terminus of peptidoglycan precursors preventing transglycosylation', 'Vancomycin binds D-Ala-D-Ala to inhibit cell wall synthesis. Its adverse effects include red man syndrome, nephrotoxicity, and ototoxicity, not myopathy with marked CK elevation.'],
      ['Inhibition of bacterial protein synthesis via binding to the 23S rRNA of the 50S ribosomal subunit', 'Linezolid binds the 23S rRNA of the 50S subunit. Its adverse effects include thrombocytopenia, peripheral/optic neuropathy, and serotonin syndrome due to weak MAO inhibition, not direct skeletal myopathy.'],
      ['Irreversible binding to the 30S ribosomal subunit causing mistranslation of bacterial proteins', 'Aminoglycosides (gentamicin, tobramycin) bind the 30S subunit. Their key toxicities are nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity/vestibulotoxicity, not skeletal myopathy.'],
    ],
    explanation: 'Daptomycin is a cyclic lipopeptide used for multi-drug resistant Gram-positive bacteremia and endocarditis. It inserts into bacterial cell membranes in a calcium-dependent manner, forming ion pores that cause rapid potassium efflux and depolarization. Its hallmark adverse effect is skeletal muscle toxicity (myositis, rhabdomyolysis), requiring regular monitoring of serum creatine kinase (CK). Daptomycin is also inactivated by pulmonary surfactant, precluding its use in pneumonia.',
    objective: 'Detail daptomycin\'s mechanism: calcium-dependent membrane channel formation leading to depolarization and potassium efflux, and recognize myopathy/rhabdomyolysis as its hallmark adverse effect.',
    takeaways: [
      'Daptomycin inserts into Gram-positive membranes via a calcium-dependent mechanism, causing potassium efflux and depolarization.',
      'Monitored with weekly CK due to myopathy/rhabdomyolysis; inactivated by lung surfactant.',
    ],
    references: [
      { label: 'Clinical Infectious Diseases 2020 Daptomycin-Induced Myopathy and Mechanism of Action', url: 'https://academic.oup.com/cid' },
    ],
    shortestValidSolution: 'MRSA therapy + high creatine kinase (myopathy) = daptomycin -> calcium-dependent bacterial membrane pore formation and potassium efflux.',
    necessaryInference: 'Identify daptomycin from MRSA bacteremia + elevated CK, then identify its calcium-dependent membrane disruption mechanism.',
    strongestDistractor: 'Binding to D-Ala-D-Ala (vancomycin), the standard MRSA first-line agent, which causes red man syndrome and nephrotoxicity rather than myopathy.',
    distractorExclusion: 'Vancomycin does not cause direct muscle injury or high CK; myopathy is the signature toxicity of daptomycin.',
