    choices: [
      ['CD40 ligand (CD154) on activated CD4+ T helper cells binding to CD40 on B cells and macrophages', 'X-linked hyper-IgM syndrome is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the CD40LG gene on the X chromosome, which encodes CD40 ligand (CD154) expressed on activated CD4+ T helper cells. CD40L binding to CD40 on B cells is strictly required for germinal center formation, activation-induced cytidine deaminase (AID) activation, and immunoglobulin heavy-chain class-switch recombination from IgM to IgG, IgA, and IgE. Without CD40L, B cells secrete only IgM. Furthermore, CD40L binding to CD40 on macrophages is essential for macrophage activation and cell-mediated immunity; its absence confers high susceptibility to opportunistic pathogens such as Pneumocystis jirovecii and Cryptosporidium parvum.'],
      ['Bruton tyrosine kinase (BTK) signaling during pre-B cell receptor signaling in the bone marrow', 'X-linked agammaglobulinemia (Bruton) involves BTK mutations, which arrest B-cell development in bone marrow, causing absent circulating B cells (CD19+ = 0) and low levels of ALL immunoglobulin classes (including IgM), without opportunistic infections like P. jirovecii.'],
      ['Adenosine deaminase (ADA) purine degradation causing toxic dATP accumulation in lymphocytes', 'ADA deficiency causes severe combined immunodeficiency (SCID), characterized by early severe lymphopenia affecting T, B, and NK cells, with markedly low absolute lymphocyte counts and failure of all immunoglobulin classes.'],
      ['WAS protein actin cytoskeleton regulation in hematopoietic lineages', 'Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome (WAS) involves WAS gene mutations, presenting with the classic triad of thrombocytopenia (microthrombocytes), eczema, and recurrent pyogenic infections, typically with elevated IgA and IgE and low IgM.'],
      ['Interleukin-12 receptor beta-1 subunit signaling impairing interferon-gamma release', 'IL-12 receptor deficiency impairs Th1 differentiation and IFN-gamma production, predisposing to disseminated mycobacterial and salmonella infections, with normal immunoglobulin class switching.'],
    ],
    explanation: 'X-linked hyper-IgM syndrome is caused by defective CD40 ligand (CD154) on activated T cells. CD40L-CD40 interaction is required for: (1) B-cell immunoglobulin class switching (resulting in high/normal IgM with absent IgG, IgA, and IgE), and (2) macrophage activation (explaining susceptibility to Pneumocystis jirovecii and Cryptosporidium). Flow cytometry shows normal B and T cell numbers, differentiating it from SCID and XLA.',
    objective: 'Recognize CD40 ligand deficiency as the cause of X-linked hyper-IgM syndrome, leading to class-switch failure and opportunistic infections (Pneumocystis, Cryptosporidium).',
    takeaways: [
      'CD40L (CD154) on T cells binds CD40 on B cells to drive class switching from IgM to IgG/IgA/IgE.',
      'CD40L is also required for macrophage activation; deficiency predisposes to Pneumocystis and Cryptosporidium.',
    ],
    references: [
      { label: 'Journal of Clinical Immunology 2020 Hyper IgM Syndromes: Clinical, Immunological, and Genetic Characteristics', url: 'https://link.springer.com/journal/10875' },
    ],
    shortestValidSolution: 'Elevated IgM + absent IgG/IgA + Pneumocystis/Cryptosporidium + normal B-cell count = CD40 ligand (CD154) deficiency.',
    necessaryInference: 'Identify CD40L defect from the pairing of class-switch failure (high IgM, low IgG/A) with opportunistic infections (macrophage impairment).',
    strongestDistractor: 'Bruton tyrosine kinase (BTK) deficiency, which also causes low IgG and IgA, but has absent B cells and profoundly low IgM.',
    distractorExclusion: 'BTK deficiency causes pan-hypogammaglobulinemia (including low IgM) and absent CD19+ B cells; hyper-IgM has elevated IgM and normal B-cell numbers.',
