    stem: 'A 32-year-old mountaineer takes a prophylactic oral medication beginning 24 hours prior to ascending to high altitude to prevent acute mountain sickness. Two days after arrival at a high-altitude base camp, he develops mild paresthesias in his fingers and toes and notices that carbonated beverages taste unpleasantly flat. Serum chemistry testing reveals: sodium 139 mEq/L, potassium 3.6 mEq/L, chloride 108 mEq/L, and bicarbonate 18 mEq/L (normal: 22–28 mEq/L). Arterial blood gas demonstrates a mild normal anion gap metabolic acidosis. Urinary pH is 7.8 (alkaline). Which of the following transport mechanisms and nephron sites is primarily inhibited by the medication this individual is taking?',
    choices: [
      ['Brush-border and cytoplasmic carbonic anhydrase in the proximal convoluted tubule', 'Acetazolamide is a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor that acts primarily in the proximal convoluted tubule. In the proximal tubule, membrane-bound carbonic anhydrase IV dehydrates luminal H2CO3 into CO2 and H2O, facilitating intracellular CO2 diffusion, where cytoplasmic carbonic anhydrase II rehydrates it to H+ and HCO3-. Intracellular bicarbonate is exported basolaterally into the blood, while H+ is pumped apically by the NHE3 exchanger to drive sodium reabsorption. Acetazolamide blocks both enzymes, preventing 85% of proximal bicarbonate reabsorption. This causes urinary bicarbonate loss (alkaline urine, pH > 7), hyperchloremic non-anion gap metabolic acidosis, and mild natriuresis. The resulting metabolic acidosis stimulates central and peripheral chemoreceptors to sustain hyperventilation at high altitude, preventing hypoxia-induced hypoventilation.'],
      ['Apical Na-Cl cotransporter (NCCT) in the early distal convoluted tubule', 'Thiazide diuretics inhibit the NCCT cotransporter in the DCT. They cause hypokalemic metabolic ALKALOSIS and hypercalcemia, not metabolic acidosis or alkaline urine.'],
      ['Apical Na-K-2Cl cotransporter (NKCC2) in the thick ascending limb of Henle', 'Loop diuretics (furosemide) inhibit NKCC2 in the TAL. They cause massive natriuresis, hypokalemic hypochloremic metabolic ALKALOSIS, and hypercalciuria, not metabolic acidosis.'],
      ['Apical epithelial sodium channels (ENaC) in the cortical collecting duct', 'Potassium-sparing diuretics (amiloride, triamterene) inhibit ENaC, causing hyperkalemia and mild metabolic acidosis, but they do not cause alkaline urine (pH 7.8) or alter taste sensation via taste bud carbonic anhydrase inhibition.'],
      ['Basolateral Na+/K+-ATPase pumps throughout the entire collecting duct system', 'Ouabain and digitalis inhibit Na+/K+-ATPase; this is not a therapeutic diuretic mechanism in clinical medicine.'],
    ],
    explanation: 'Acetazolamide inhibits carbonic anhydrase in the brush border and cytoplasm of the proximal convoluted tubule. This prevents bicarbonate reabsorption, producing alkaline urine (bicarbonaturia) and hyperchloremic non-anion gap metabolic acidosis. The resulting systemic acidosis stimulates ventilation, which treats/prevents acute mountain sickness. Inhibition of carbonic anhydrase in taste buds also causes the characteristic alteration in carbonated beverage taste.',
    objective: 'Explain the mechanism of acetazolamide: proximal tubular carbonic anhydrase inhibition causing bicarbonaturia and normal anion gap metabolic acidosis.',
    takeaways: [
      'Acetazolamide inhibits proximal tubule carbonic anhydrase, blocking bicarbonate reabsorption.',
      'Produces alkaline urine, non-anion gap metabolic acidosis, and altered carbonation taste; used for acute mountain sickness and glaucoma.',
    ],
    references: [
      { label: 'Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology 15th ed. Chapter 15: Diuretic Agents', url: 'https://accessmedicine.mhmedical.com/' },
    ],
    shortestValidSolution: 'Altitude prophylaxis + alkaline urine (pH 7.8) + metabolic acidosis + altered carbonation taste = acetazolamide -> proximal tubule carbonic anhydrase inhibition.',
    necessaryInference: 'Identify acetazolamide from the classic altitude indication and beverage taste change, then identify its proximal tubular carbonic anhydrase target.',
